== The Ancient Law and Unique Bond of Marriage

=== §. 79. #emph[Of the ancient law of marriage.]

#align(center)[
  #block(width: 85%, breakable: false)[
    #set par(justify: false)
    #text(size: 0.9em, weight: 600)[Eph 5:31.]

    #text(style: "italic")[For this reason a man will leave his father and mother and be joined to his wife, and the two will be one flesh.]
  ]
]

#v(0.8em)

#smallcaps[The] same points that were laid down before, concerning the near union of man and wife and of Christ and the church, are here further confirmed by the ancient law of marriage. The apostle mentions it all the more because it follows on the text to which he alluded in the previous verse. For when Moses had recorded these words of Adam concerning Eve, “This is now bone of my bones and flesh of my flesh,”#footnote[Gen 2:23, 24.] he adds this law: “For this reason a man will leave his father,” etc. In this place these words have both a #emph[literal] and a #emph[mystical] sense: a #emph[literal] sense of man and wife, a #emph[mystical] sense of Christ and the church. The main thing the apostle aims at is to #footnote[Inference.] show how closely man and wife are linked together, so that they may be moved all the more to perform the mutual duties, and the duties proper to each, which they owe one another. But he had set before husbands and wives the examples of Christ and the church, as patterns and motives to do their duty. So he applies what was first spoken of man and wife to Christ and his church, to show that since there is so fit a likeness between these two couples, the pattern set before them is all the more to the purpose, and the reason drawn from it all the more forcible.

Because explaining the literal sense will give great light to the mystery, I will first handle this text according to the meaning of the letter.

The first clause #emph[(for this reason)] implies a necessary connection #footnote[ἀντὶ τούτου.] with what went before. The near union of man and wife, as well as of Christ and his church, was noted before. A wife was said to be as the #emph[body]#footnote[#emph[v.] 28.] of a man, indeed as #emph[himself.] Adam#footnote[Gen 2:23.] called her his #emph[flesh and bones.] From this both Moses and Paul draw the inference, “Therefore,” or, “For this reason a man will leave father.” Because man and wife are so near by God’s institution, they must also be most dear to each other in their mutual affection.

The #emph[man] (meaning a husband) is mentioned here in particular, #footnote[Meaning of the words.] because when this law was first made, the woman was brought to him to see how he would like her. Having set his affection on her, he was to be bound by this law to continue that good liking toward her. He is also mentioned because of the preeminence which a man has above his wife. Yet the man is not the only one bound by it; the wife is bound too. The nature and rule of a relation require as much: if a man must hold fast to his wife inseparably, the wife must likewise hold fast to her husband.

These words #emph[(will leave father and mother)] are not to be taken #emph[generally,] of all duties, as if children owed no duty to their parents after they are married. Nor are they to be taken #emph[simply,] as if parents were really to be utterly forsaken. They are meant:

1\. Of the #emph[daily service] which children under their parents’ government give them, seeking to please them in all things. When children are married, their daily attendance must be on their wives or husbands,#footnote[1 Cor 7:33, 34.] taking care how to please them.

2\. Of #emph[setting up a new family.] For this purpose their parents’ house must be left, and the husband and wife must live together.

3\. Of the #emph[distinction to be made between parents and wife or husband.] Suppose some unavoidable occasion should arise in which a man must leave either his parent or his wife: for instance, his parent and his wife are both dying, in places so far apart that the husband cannot possibly be with both, and yet both urgently want him with them. By this law he must leave his parent and hold fast to his wife.

By this, then, the bond of marriage is declared to be the most inviolable bond there can be. For all men know that the bond between parent and child is a firm and inviolable bond. But the bond between husband and wife is more firm and inviolable.

To set forth the firmness of the marriage bond he adds this emphatic phrase, #emph[be joined] (or, as the word properly means according to its root, #emph[be glued)] #footnote[προσκολληθήσεται. κόλλα. Glue.] to his wife. Things that are well glued together are as fast, firm and close as if they were one whole piece. Indeed, we see by experience that a table will often split in the solid wood before it will come apart where it is glued. So a husband ought to be as firm to his wife as to himself, and she to him.

This fits well with what follows #emph[(the two will be one flesh).] Our English cannot well express the Greek #footnote[ἔσονται οἱ δύο εἰς σάρκα μίαν.] word for word in good sense (word for word it is #emph[the two shall be into,] or #emph[in, one flesh).] The meaning is this: those who were two before marriage are brought into one flesh by the bond of marriage, to be just as one flesh, as closely united as the parts of the same body and the same flesh. This unity is not in respect of the bodily union of the two (for once they are married they are #emph[one flesh,] #footnote[#emph[Matrimonium non facit coitus, sed voluntas.] \[It is not intercourse but consent that makes a marriage.\] #emph[Chrysost. in Mat. hom.] 32.] even if they never know one another). Nor is it in respect of procreation, because one child comes from them both (for even if they never have a child, they are still one flesh). It is in respect of God’s institution, who has laid it down for a law, and as another nature, that man and wife should be this near to one another. Their consent in marriage (by virtue of God’s institution) makes them #emph[one flesh.]

Our English rightly brings out the emphasis of the original in this word THE #emph[(the two).] It shows that the bond #footnote[οἱ δύο.] of marriage knits only #emph[two] together: one man and one woman, and no more.

This law sets forth the #emph[union between man and wife.] #footnote[Sum.]

In it three things are noted concerning the state of marriage.

1\. Its preeminence #emph[(a man will leave his father and mother).]

2\. Its firmness #emph[(and be joined to his wife).]

3\. Its nearness #emph[(the two will be one flesh).]

=== §. 80. #emph[Of preferring husband or wife to parents.]

The first point shows that

#emph[A wife or a husband must be preferred before parents.] The #footnote[Doctrine.] examples of Leah#footnote[Gen 31:14.] and Rachel, and of Michal#footnote[1 Sam 19:11.] too, are commendable in this respect.

1\. The bond of marriage is more ancient, more firm and more #footnote[Reason.] near. There were husband and wife before there were parent and child. And there is a time when parents and children may part from one another, even while both are living. But there is no time in which man and wife may part from each other until death parts them. And children, though they come from the flesh of their parents, are yet made two (so that #emph[of one come two).] But husbands and wives, though they were two before, are yet made one (so that #emph[of two comes one).]

What wrong, then, do those parents do to their children who #footnote[Use.] keep them under such strict subjection, even after they are married, #footnote[Parents may not keep their married children too strictly.] that they cannot freely do the duty they ought to their husband or their wife! This goes beyond what a parent’s authority reaches to. Yet many think that their children owe them as much service after they are married as before. This is directly against this law.

The wrong is greater, and the practice more sinful, of #footnote[Nor keep them from husband or wife.] those who keep their children from their husbands or their wives. “The match,” they say, “has turned out much worse than we expected.” But this should have been looked into more carefully beforehand. After marriage it is too late to seek such a remedy.

On the other side, there are many children who so regard #footnote[Children may not prefer their parents before husband or wife. #emph[Apud Romanos sponsa obtecta flammeo capite quum extulisset pedem è paternis aedibus, vertebatur in gyrum, itaque ducebatur in domum sponsi, nesciret redeundi viam ad aedes parentum. Erasm.] \[Among the Romans the bride, her head covered with a flame-colored veil, when she had set her foot out of her father’s house, was turned round about, and so led into the bridegroom’s house, that she might not know the way back to her parents’ house.\]] their parents that they neglect their husband or their wife. Some husbands will give what they can to their parents and keep their wives very bare, letting them go without necessities. They do not care how they vex and grieve their wives, so long as they please their parents. Some wives also will secretly steal from their husbands to give to their parents.

Others can never stay away from their parents’ houses, but go there as often as they can. To show how unfitting this was, the ancient Romans had a custom of covering the bride’s face with a yellow veil. As soon as she was out of her father’s house they turned her round and round, and so carried her to her husband’s house, so that she would not know the way back to her father’s house. All such pretenses of love to parents are more perverse than pious, and in such people natural affection has more sway than true religion. Their pretense of piety toward parents is no just excuse for the injury they do to husband or wife.

=== §. 81. #emph[Of the firmness of the matrimonial bond.]

The second point, concerning the firmness of the marriage knot, in these words #emph[(and be joined to his wife),] yields two doctrines.

1\. #emph[Man and wife must keep company together by continual] #footnote[See #emph[Treatise] 2, #emph[part] 2, §. 14.] #emph[cohabitation.] For this end they leave their parents’ family and set up a new family.

2\. #emph[Man and wife are joined together by an inviolable bond.] It #footnote[See #emph[Treatise] 2, #emph[part] 2, §. 2.] must never be cut apart until death cuts it. Body and soul must be separated from one another before husband and wife are.

Be careful, therefore, to preserve this indissoluble knot. And live together in such a way that you#super[+] may live together with comfort, because you must not part.

=== §. 82. #emph[Of only two being joined together in marriage.]

The third point, concerning the nearness of man and wife, in these words #emph[(the two will be one flesh),] yields two more doctrines.

1\. #emph[Marriage can be only between two,] one man and one woman. For it is impossible that more than two should be joined together as nearly and firmly as man and wife are. Almost every word in this law proves this doctrine. For it says #emph[a man,] not men; to #emph[a wife,] not to wives; to #emph[his] wife, not to another man’s wife; #emph[two,] not more than two; #emph[the] two, not any two; #emph[one flesh,] not many fleshes.

#emph[Objection.] This word #emph[(two)] is not in the law as Moses records #footnote[Gen 2:24.] it.

#emph[Answer.] It is necessarily implied there, for at that time there were only two in the world. God, speaking of them then, means only two. The same Spirit who guided Moses guided the #footnote[Matt 19:5.] evangelists and the #footnote[1 Cor 6:16.] apostles too. So, since they put in this word #emph[(two),] it is certain that Moses intended it, just as with the word #emph[(only)] which Christ puts into this text: “serve Him only.”#footnote[Deut 6:13. Matt 4:10.]

#emph[Question.] Why did God at first make only one man and one woman?

#emph[Answer.] The #footnote[Mal 2:15.] prophet answers: “Because He seeks godly offspring.”

If, therefore, there are more than two, the offspring that comes from it is an adulterous offspring.

=== §. 83. #emph[Of polygamy and bigamy.]

Can polygamy (having many wives) or bigamy (having two wives at once) have any good warrant against so express a law? Are not both of them against the first institution of marriage, so that we may say, “It was not this way from the beginning”?#footnote[Matt 19:8.] Are they not also against #footnote[Deut 17:17. Lev 18:18.] other particular laws? Lamech,#footnote[Gen 4:19. #emph[Magis pertinere ad nuptiarum bonum, non vnum & multas, sed vnum & vnam, satis indicat ipsa prima diuinitus facta coniugum copula, etc.] \[That one man and one woman, not one man and many women, belongs more to the good of marriage, the very first joining of a couple, made by God, shows clearly enough, etc.\] #emph[Aug. de Nupt. & concup. l.] 1. #emph[c.] 9. Gen 2:18. Gen 16:5; 21:11.] one of Cain’s cursed line, was the first we read of who presumed to go against that ancient law.

#emph[Objection.] Afterward many patriarchs and other saints took that liberty for themselves.

#emph[Answer.] It was their sin, and a great blemish in them. The common error of the time, and their insatiable desire for many children, made them fall into it. Many troubles followed from it. Nor can anyone think otherwise than that much harm must follow from having more wives than one. For whereas God at first made a wife to be a help to man, two or more wives cannot but be a great grief and vexation to him because of the rivalry between them. Through Hagar Sarah was stirred up against Abraham, and Abraham was grieved at Sarah’s words. Though Leah and Rachel were sisters, their rivalry was great. The same is noted of Peninnah and many others.

Considering how heinous this sin is, our laws #footnote[#emph[Statute] 1 #emph[James I,] c. 11.] have justly made it a felony for a man to have more wives than one, or a woman more husbands.

=== §. 84. #emph[Of the close union of man and wife.]

2\. #emph[Husband and wife are the nearest of all to one another.] #footnote[Doctrine.] Every clause in the law named above proves as much.

1\. #emph[Parents] must be #emph[left] for a wife. Who are nearer than parent and child? If man and wife are nearer than the nearest, then they are the nearest of all.

2\. A man is #emph[glued] to his wife. This metaphor sets forth the #emph[nearness] of a thing as well as its #emph[firmness,] for things glued together are as one whole thing.

3\. Man and wife are #emph[one flesh.] Many from one are made two, but no #emph[two] are made #emph[one] so nearly and truly as man and wife.

Things are to be reckoned as near and as united as God has set them. #footnote[Reason.] But God has knit man and wife together this nearly, and made them one flesh. “What #footnote[Matt 19:6.] God has joined together,” says Christ of man and wife. In this respect the marriage union is called “the covenant #footnote[Prov 2:17.] of her God.” So this covenant cannot be released by anyone, no, not by the mutual consent of man and wife (“What God has joined together, let man not separate”). Yet many other covenants made between one party and another may be released and annulled by the mutual consent of both parties.

1\. This shows that the transgressions of man and wife #footnote[Uses.] against one another are the most heinous of all, more than those of #footnote[1\. Mutual transgressions of man and wife most heinous.] a friend, companion, brother, child, parent or anyone else. Who would not cry #emph[shame on] the child who hates his parent, or #emph[shame on] the parent who hates his child? The heathen and savages would not think them worthy of human society. What then may be thought of the man who hates his wife, or the wife who hates her husband? Apply this to all other transgressions, and note well how “the #smallcaps[Lord] has been a witness” to them. #footnote[Mal 2:14.]

2\. This also shows what a monstrous thing it is to sow #footnote[2\. Monstrous to stir up strife between man and wife.] any seeds of discord, and stir up strife between man and wife. Those who do it are the devil’s instruments in it, and a devilish spirit is in them. For Satan labors most to undo those knots which the Lord ties most firmly. Children of different mothers, and the separate friends of each party, are much at fault in this. “May they be cursed in the presence of the #smallcaps[Lord],” every one of them.

3\. This#footnote[1 Sam 26:19.] near conjunction between man and wife is a great #footnote[3\. All duties between man and wife to be cheerfully performed. Isa 58:7.] motive to stir them both up to perform cheerfully all the duties which God requires of either of them. For by doing them they do their duty and show kindness to their own flesh. No man may turn away from #emph[his own flesh] in the wider sense; that is, no man may neglect any duty of mercy or justice to his neighbor who is of the same stock as he is. Shall a husband or wife, then, turn away from one another, who are #emph[one flesh] in the nearest respect that can possibly be? Not because they come #emph[from] one flesh, but because they come #footnote[εἰς σάρκα μίαν.] #emph[into] one flesh.

So much for the literal sense of this verse.

The mystical sense follows.

=== §. 85. #emph[Of the marriage union of Christ and the church.]

The ancient marriage law named above is here applied mystically #footnote[#emph[Quod per historiam impletum est in Adam, per prophetiam significat Christum qui reliquit patrem, etc.] \[What was fulfilled as history in Adam signifies by prophecy Christ, who left his Father, etc.\] #emph[Aug. de Gen. lib.] 2. #emph[c.] 24.] to Christ and the church. This is evident from the next verse, where the apostle, referring to this verse, says, “This is a great mystery.” There is, then, a mystery contained in it. But of what, or of whom, is that mystery? The apostle himself answers in these words: “I am speaking about Christ and the church.”

The mystery in general is this:

#block(inset: (x: 1em))[#emph[Christ and the church are to one another as husband and wife.]]

The particulars of this mystery are these.

The matrimonial conjunction between Christ and the church is a most preeminent, firm and near conjunction.

First, of the general.

The many titles of betrothal and marriage which Scripture gives to Christ and the church in their relation to one another clearly show that they are joined together by the honorable, inseparable and inviolable bond of marriage. He is called a #emph[bridegroom,]#footnote[John 3:29.] she a #emph[bride;] he the #emph[beloved,]#footnote[Song 1:13, 15.] she his #emph[love;] he a #emph[husband,]#footnote[2 Cor 11:2.] she a #emph[wife;] he a #emph[head,]#footnote[Eph 5:23.] she the #emph[body;] both #emph[one flesh.]#footnote[#emph[and v.] 31.]

2\. All the things required to join man and wife together fitly come together between Christ and the church.

1\. They are persons fit to be joined. Though Christ is #footnote[The things which make Christ and the Church fit to be husband and wife.] God, yet for #footnote[John 17:19.] this end he became man. And though the church was impure, yet for this end she is cleansed and sanctified.

2\. They have their parents’ consent, for God is the #footnote[John 20:17.] common Father of both. And God has given #footnote[Rom 8:32.] Christ to the church, and the #footnote[John 6:39.] church to Christ.

3\. They have given their #footnote[Song 2:16.] mutual consent to each other.

4\. He #footnote[Eph 5:23, 24.] bears a husband’s affection toward her, and she is willing to yield a wife’s subjection to him.

5\. He has #footnote[Eph 4:8.] given her many favors and gifts as pledges of his love. And she, in testimony of her faithfulness, was circumcised under the law and is baptized under the gospel, and binds herself with all the sacred bonds and covenants which God has sanctified for that purpose.

6\. He has prepared #footnote[John 14:3.] dwelling places for them both together, and #footnote[Rev 22:17, 20.] she earnestly desires to be with him.

Here is another evidence of Christ’s wonderful love to the #footnote[1\. #emph[Use.]] church, and of the near union between Christ and her. The #footnote[A high favor to be the spouse of Christ.] former was that she was his #emph[body;] this, that she is his #emph[wife.] The church might well say, as Abigail did, “Here is your maidservant, ready to serve and to wash the feet of my #footnote[1 Sam 25:41.] lord’s servants,” and, as the prodigal son did, “Make me like one of your hired #footnote[Luke 15:19.] servants,” or, as the Baptist did: “… the straps of whose sandals I am not worthy to stoop down and #footnote[Mark 1:7.] untie.” What a favor it is, then, to be made his spouse, his wife, his queen! Great was the favor which Ahasuerus showed to Esther when he made her his wife. He was a great monarch, who reigned “over 127 provinces from India to Cush,” but Esther was a poor orphan and a captive. Yet this favor was not comparable to Christ’s. For there was no such disparity and inequality between Ahasuerus and Esther as between Christ and the church. Nor is Esther’s advancement to be compared with the church’s. And yet there was some cause in Esther to move Ahasuerus to do what he did, for she was very beautiful and lovely, and worthy to be loved. But #footnote[§. 33, 34.] in the church, when Christ first set his love on her, there was no such thing. No example of love can be given that is in any way comparable to this.

Let the church, therefore, and all who profess themselves #footnote[2\. #emph[Use.]] to be of the church, take such notice of this that they may endeavor #footnote[#emph[Direction.] How Christ’s spouse must conduct herself.] to conduct themselves worthily of this honor and advancement. They must not grow proud and insolent because of it, but must despise all vain and worldly trifles. They must answer love with love, as the church is set forth in Solomon’s Song; be subject to her husband, reverence and obey him, and perform all the duties belonging to such a wife; and seek by all good means to maintain the honor of her place. The church is made a pattern of duty to all wives. If she should fail, greater harm would follow from it than from Vashti’s #footnote[Esth 1:16. #emph[etc.]] disobedience.

This is all the more to be heeded because it is not only a #footnote[3\. #emph[Use.]] matter of instruction but also of trial. It shows both what those #footnote[#emph[Trial.] Who are Christ’s spouse.] who are of the church ought to do and #footnote[§. 24.] what in fact they will do. Therefore no profane person who thinks lightly of the Lord Jesus, no idolater who sets his love on other husbands, no swearer or blasphemer who dishonors the great name of Jesus, none who are in any way rebellious against him, and none who hate, scorn, scoff at or hurt any of his members, can have any comfort in this advancement of the church, because they have no part in it, nor right to it.

But great is the comfort which the true saints may receive #footnote[4\. #emph[Use.]] from it. For by virtue of this matrimonial bond, #footnote[#emph[Consolation.] The privileges of Christ’s spouse.]

1\. Christ is made a yokefellow with his church, and her companion. Under all the burdens laid on her he puts his shoulder, to make them the easier. Indeed, he has so taken on himself the great burdens of God’s wrath, the curse of the law, and sin, the cause of it, that he has completely freed his church from them, because otherwise they would have crushed her down to hell.

2\. Christ is as her champion, to answer all challenges sent to her; as her advocate, to plead against and answer all the complaints that will be made against her; and as her surety, to discharge all her debts. The church being under Christ’s covering, as a wife is under her husband’s in law, so that he answers for her, he is as her very self, all in all for her and to her.

3\. All his honors, goods and privileges are hers. She has #footnote[#emph[Ecclesia omnem sponsi potestatem tenet.] \[The church holds all the power of her bridegroom.\] #emph[Aug. contr. Don. l.] 4. #emph[c.] 1.] a right to them, and her share in them. She is a co-heir with him (Rom 8:17), a queen because he is a king (Ps 45:9), and all glorious, as was noted on v. 27.

4\. He will surely perform all the duties of a husband: to love her, bear with her, provide for her, and the like. He is able to do all, for he is omnipotent. He must also be willing, because he has willingly taken this place on himself. He has made himself a pattern to other husbands; will he not, then, do himself what he requires of others?

If ever any wife might take comfort in a match, the church may take comfort in this match.

The benefit of this match will appear yet more vividly by considering in particular the three properties of this matrimonial bond named above: its #emph[preeminence, firmness] and #emph[nearness.]

=== §. 86. #emph[Of Christ’s leaving his Father and mother for his spouse.]

I. The preeminence of the matrimonial bond between Christ and the church appears in this, that

#emph[Christ left his Father and his mother for his spouse, the church.] As Christ is God, God is his Father; as man, the Virgin Mary was his mother. Now his leaving his Father must be taken only by way of likeness, in that he came from the place where his Father dwells to the place where his spouse was. The Scripture says that he was “at the Father’s side,”#footnote[John 1:18.] “at His side … His delight day by day, rejoicing always #footnote[Prov 8:30.] in His presence.” Yet “He also descended to the lower parts of the earth,” #footnote[Eph 4:9.] where his spouse was. He “came from the Father#footnote[John 16:28.] and entered the world.”

But he truly and properly preferred his spouse before his mother. For when he was instructing his spouse, and his mother came to interrupt him, he said to his mother, “Who is My #footnote[Mark 3:33, 34.] mother?” and to his spouse, “Here is My mother!”

The #footnote[Use.] church, and all who are of the church, must be of the same mind toward Christ. She must forget her own people and her father’s #footnote[#emph[Imitation.] All to be forsaken for Christ. Ps 45:10.] house. Since Christ has gone before us and given us so good an example, what a height of ingratitude it would be for us to prefer father, mother or anyone else before Christ our husband! Note what he says in this case: “Anyone who loves #footnote[Matt 10:37.] his father or mother more than Me is not worthy of Me.” And again: “If anyone comes to Me and does not hate his father and mother …, he cannot #footnote[Luke 14:26 expounded. How parents are to be hated for Christ.] be My disciple.” To #emph[hate] here is to be so far from preferring father and mother before Christ that one would rather hate father and mother than not love Christ. Or it is to love Christ so wholly above all that our love of parents, compared with it, is a hatred. So Levi “said of his father and mother, ‘I do not consider them’ … #footnote[Deut 33:9.] for he kept Your word and maintained Your covenant.”

This, then, is our duty: that we do not let any natural affection and doting on our parents swallow up the love we owe to Christ, as Pharaoh’s “cows that were sickly and thin devoured #footnote[Gen 41:4.] the seven sleek, well-fed cows.” How much less should any love of this world, of its profits, promotions or pleasures, draw our hearts away from Christ! Should we not rather say and do as the apostles did: “Look, we have left #footnote[Matt 19:27.] everything to follow You”?

=== §. 87. #emph[Of the indissoluble union between Christ and the church.]

#smallcaps[II]. The firmness of the bond by which Christ and the church are said to be #emph[glued] together is greater and more inviolable than that by which man and wife are joined together. Death parts man and wife, but death cannot make a separation between Christ and the church. So from this metaphor we may well infer that #emph[Christ and the church are inseparably] #footnote[Hos 2:19.] #emph[knit together.] “I will betroth you to Me forever,” says Christ to the church. The covenant which Christ makes with his church is an #emph[everlasting] covenant. The mountains will be removed #footnote[Isa 61:8; 54:10.] and the hills shaken before his loving devotion departs from the church.

The steadfastness and unchangeableness of his will is the #footnote[Reason.] only cause of this. Whom he loves, he loves “to the very end.” #footnote[John 13:1.] “God’s gifts and His call are irrevocable.”#footnote[Rom 11:29.] He is not like the hard-hearted Jews, who would put away their wives on every slight occasion. “I hate divorce,” says the #smallcaps[Lord].#footnote[Mal 2:16.] Therefore, though the church through her weakness departs from him and plays the harlot, “yet return to Me,”#footnote[Jer 3:1.] declares the #smallcaps[Lord].

Let us learn by this pattern to hold fast to the Lord. #footnote[#emph[Use.] Hold fast to Christ.] This is a duty most due to Christ, who holds so fast to us, and it is therefore often #footnote[Deut 10:20; 13:4. Acts 11:23.] expressed in the Scripture.

There are three virtues of special use for this purpose: faith, hope and love.

#emph[Faith] is the hand by which we lay fast hold on Christ and, as it were, knit him to ourselves, as he by his Spirit knits us to himself. This makes us rest and rely on him for all needful things, and not leave him for anything.

#emph[Hope] is the anchor which holds us fast against all the storms of Satan, so that they can never drive us out of our harbor, which is the Lord Jesus Christ.

#emph[Love] is the glue and solder which makes us one with Christ. For it is the property of love to unite in one those who love one another. “The souls of Jonathan and #footnote[1 Sam 18:1.] David were knit together.” Why? “Jonathan loved him as himself.” He who loves is well pleased with the one he loves, and also seeks to please him, so that they may delight in one another. If these three virtues were well rooted in us, we would say, “Who shall separate us from the love of Christ?#footnote[Rom 8:35. #emph[etc.]] Shall trouble or distress,” etc.

=== §. 88. #emph[Of the equal privilege of all the saints.]

#smallcaps[III]. Concerning the phrase by which the nearness of #footnote[All the saints made one spouse.] man and wife is set forth #emph[(the two will be one flesh),] it may be asked how this can be applied to Christ and the saints, who are more than two.

#emph[Answer.] Christ by one Spirit knits us all into one body, #footnote[1 Cor 12:13.] and so makes all of us, taken together, one spouse. The multitude of saints no more implies many wives than the multitude of members which a wife’s natural body has. This point, then, teaches us that

#emph[In the mystical marriage between Christ and the church, each and every one of the saints has an equal privilege.] Some are not #footnote[See §. 22.] concubines and some wives, nor are some more loved or preferred above others, but all are one wife. “All one in Christ Jesus.” #footnote[Gal 3:28.]

Neither the Father who gave them all, nor the Son who #footnote[Reason.] took them all, saw anything in one more than in another; their pure grace moved them to do what they did. Every one may well apply all the privileges named above to themselves, and not one envy another.

This gives instruction to the more eminent in the #footnote[Use.] church, that they should not, like proud ladies, lord it over others as if they were their maidservants. And it gives consolation to the lowlier sort, that they may hold themselves up and possess their souls with patience, and not envy or grieve at the outward prosperity and privileges of others. In the greatest privilege they are equal to the greatest.

So much for the parties joined to Christ. For these words #emph[(the two)] show that all the saints are only #emph[one;] Christ is the other of the two. The next words #emph[(will be one flesh)] show how near those saints are to Christ.

=== §. 89. #emph[Of the close union between Christ and the church.]

The main point to be noted here is that

#emph[Christ and the church are most nearly linked together.] What #footnote[#emph[Christus nihil aliud deputat corpus suum (i.) Ecclesiam, quam seipsum, quia de Christo & ecclesia verius intelligitur, Erunt duo in carne una.] \[Christ counts his body, that is, the church, as nothing other than himself, because “The two shall be in one flesh” is more truly understood of Christ and the church.\] #emph[Aug. de pec. mer. lib.] 1. #emph[cap.] 31.] can be nearer than for two to come #emph[into one flesh?]

This is something more than to be #emph[of Christ’s flesh.] That shows that we are, as it were, cut out of Christ; this shows that we are knit to him again. That was a preparation for this; this is, as it were, the consummation and perfection of all. #footnote[See §. 70.] Many metaphors are used to set forth the near union between Christ and his church, but this surpasses them all. As here we and Christ are said to be #emph[one flesh,] so in another place we are said to be #emph[one spirit.] Well, therefore, might the name and title #emph[Christ] be given to this spouse of Christ.

It#footnote[§. 84.] was noted on a ground like this that, of all other persons, #footnote[1 Cor 6:17; 12:12.] the transgression of a wife against her husband is the most heinous. What, then, are the transgressions of the church against Christ? We are much more bound to Christ for the privileges we receive from him as a #emph[head] and #emph[husband,] our Savior having made an everlasting covenant of marriage with us, than for those we received from him as our Creator, Lord and master. So the rebellions now committed against him are the more monstrous. To Adam, who broke the first covenant, and so sinned against his father and master like a rebellious child and servant, mercy and pardon were given. But for those who now break the bond of this everlasting covenant, and make a total and final desertion, utterly renouncing this husband, or by their adultery causing him to give them a certificate of divorce, “no further sacrifice #footnote[Heb 10:27.] for sins remains, but only a fearful expectation of judgment and of raging fire that will consume all adversaries.” This is to be noted to make us the more watchful over our ways. We must resist sin at its beginning; we must “see to it that no #footnote[Heb 12:15.] one falls away from the grace of God”; and we must “not give #footnote[Eph 4:27.] the devil a foothold” at all. Satan will strive most to dissolve the nearest bonds that God makes. Since this is the nearest of all, we ought to be most careful to preserve it.
